MOGRI
Primitive 94, CSP-106

Definition:

Mogri is undifferentiated potential held before a required distinction is fixed.

It is the minimal pre-container condition in which an important object, intent, possibility, or unresolved relation can remain available without being forced into a category, answer, role, cause, or final interpretation.

Mogri does not generate the later distinction.
Mogri does not decide what the object will become.
Mogri does not require that the object ever become differentiated.

Mogri holds the possibility space before commitment.

Mogri is not:

- an entity
- an actor
- a force
- a cause
- a hidden memory store
- a collection of unknown facts
- a synonym for confusion
- a synonym for mystery
- a requirement to remain unresolved forever

Mogri can contain an object whose value is unknown, unspecified, disputed, incomplete, or not yet usefully divided, but only when that unresolved state matters to the active object.

Simple English:

Mogri is the part that lets an important thing stay unresolved without making up the answer.

Example:

```text
A -> MOGRI -> B
```

A no longer determines the object.
B does not determine it yet.
Mogri is the held interval.

Mogri can also be the starting condition:

```text
MOGRI -> first differentiation -> later state
```

The general form is:

```text
before forced distinction = MOGRI
after sufficient distinction = differentiated state
```

Operational note:

A system is behaving in a Mogri-compatible way when it preserves an important unresolved object without silently replacing it, dropping it, or resolving it without support.

A system is not demonstrating Mogri merely because it can list unknowns or unfinished tasks.

The relevant test is whether the active unresolved object remains available and intact until a real distinction is justified.

Claim boundary:

This definition describes the role of the primitive.

It does not prove a hidden internal container or mechanism in any model.
